hidclass: a device that carries motors and nothing else is not a HID controller.

mgvf-0011 wrote a promise into its own header: "the device is not replaced,
wrapped or hidden: it keeps GUID_DEVINTERFACE_HID and every client that reads it
today reads the same bytes tomorrow." That was the right promise for a pad. It
was the wrong one for THIS device, and the difference has cost two releases.

hidclass makes one device per top-level collection, so mgvf-0010's haptics
collection arrives on a PDO of its own -- carrying the pad's vendor and product
ids, because it is the same physical pad on the same bus. A client that finds
its controller by walking the HID interface and reading those ids therefore
sees TWO DualSense, and the second one answers nothing a DualSense would.

Sony's own library does exactly that. Its import table is HidD_GetHidGuid,
SETUPAPI and HidD_GetAttributes, and the disassembly in
docs/what-libscepad-does-to-the-motors.md has the rest. NO TITLE USING THAT
LIBRARY STARTS while XInputRumble is on. It has been known since 2026-09-09 and
shipped as a documented limitation twice.

IT COSTS NOTHING TO FIX, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT WORTH DOING, because
nothing else ever wanted this device. xinput does not look for it under the HID
interface: dlls/xinput1_3/main.c enumerates GUID_DEVINTERFACE_WINEXINPUT, which
mgvf-0011 registers and which this patch does not touch. Every other client was
only ever going to be confused by it -- which is why mgvf-0010 already declared
it a multi-axis controller rather than a gamepad, to keep games that enumerate
pads by usage from counting it. This finishes that thought for clients that
enumerate by vendor id instead, which usage cannot hide from.

THE TEST IS THREE CONDITIONS AND EACH IS DOING WORK, because getting this wrong
in the permissive direction would make a real force-feedback controller vanish
from every application on the system:

  MULTI_AXIS_CONTROLLER    the usage mgvf-0010 chose for this device on purpose.
                           A real pad or wheel declares GAMEPAD or JOYSTICK.
  a haptics collection     it has motors to offer at all.
  NO generic-desktop axes  mgvf-0010 puts this device's single input byte on the
                           VENDOR page precisely so nothing reads it as an axis.
                           Anything with a stick, a pedal or a wheel declares
                           axes here and is a real controller whatever its usage
                           says.

A device that is all three is one this driver built for one client. It is
offered to that client and to nobody else.

NOT CLAIMED: that this makes those titles rumble through XInput. They do not
need to -- a game that links Sony's library drives the pad itself and already
rumbles over Bluetooth on mgvf-0002 through mgvf-0004. What this removes is the
reason they refused to start at all, so the switch stops being a thing a person
has to know to turn off for half their library.

CONFIRMED THE SAME DAY. Mortal Shell 2 -- the title that would not start with
XInputRumble on, and the one this limitation was written around twice -- starts
with it on. That is one title and one library, which is the whole of what was
broken: the block was libScePad's enumeration finding two devices with the pad's
ids, and there is now one.

What is NOT claimed from that run is any of the rest of it. The three conditions
were still read from mgvf-0010's descriptor rather than exercised against a real
force-feedback controller, because there is none here to try; a wheel or a pad
that declares its own axes and its own motors is what would prove the narrow
half of this patch, and nothing on this machine is one.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/hidclass.sys/hid.h
+++ b/dlls/hidclass.sys/hid.h
@@ -93,6 +93,7 @@
     /* MGVF mgvf-0011: a pad that carries a HID haptics collection is also
      * offered under the private interface xinput looks for. See pnp.c. */
     BOOL is_xinput_haptics;
+    BOOL is_motors_only; /* MGVF mgvf-0028: exists to carry motors and nothing else, so it is not offered as a HID device */
     UNICODE_STRING xinput_link_name;
 };
 
--- a/dlls/hidclass.sys/pnp.c
+++ b/dlls/hidclass.sys/pnp.c
@@ -216,6 +216,47 @@
     return found;
 }
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0028: a device that exists ONLY to carry motors, as opposed to a
+ * controller that also has them. The difference decides whether it is offered
+ * to everything that reads HID or only to xinput, and getting it wrong in the
+ * permissive direction is how a real force-feedback controller would vanish
+ * from every application on the system. So all three of these are required and
+ * each is doing work:
+ *
+ *   MULTI_AXIS_CONTROLLER   mgvf-0010 chose that usage for this device on
+ *                           purpose, so that a game enumerating gamepads and
+ *                           joysticks does not count it as a second controller.
+ *                           A real pad or wheel declares GAMEPAD or JOYSTICK.
+ *   a haptics collection    it has motors to offer at all.
+ *   NO generic-desktop axes mgvf-0010 puts this device's single input byte on
+ *                           the VENDOR page precisely so that nothing reads it
+ *                           as an axis. Anything with a stick, a pedal or a
+ *                           wheel declares axes here and is a real controller,
+ *                           whatever its usage says.
+ *
+ * A device that is all three is one this driver built, and the only client that
+ * has any business with it is xinput. */
+static BOOL is_motors_only_collection( HIDP_COLLECTION_DESC *desc )
+{
+    HIDP_VALUE_CAPS *values;
+    HIDP_CAPS caps;
+    USHORT count;
+    BOOL axes = FALSE;
+    ULONG i;
+
+    if (desc->UsagePage != HID_USAGE_PAGE_GENERIC ||
+        desc->Usage != HID_USAGE_GENERIC_MULTI_AXIS_CONTROLLER) return FALSE;
+    if (!has_haptics_collection( desc )) return FALSE;
+    if (HidP_GetCaps( desc->PreparsedData, &caps ) != HIDP_STATUS_SUCCESS) return FALSE;
+    if (!(count = caps.NumberInputValueCaps)) return TRUE;
+    if (!(values = malloc( count * sizeof(*values) ))) return FALSE;
+    if (HidP_GetValueCaps( HidP_Input, values, &count, desc->PreparsedData ) == HIDP_STATUS_SUCCESS)
+        for (i = 0; i < count && !axes; ++i)
+            axes = values[i].UsagePage == HID_USAGE_PAGE_GENERIC;
+    free( values );
+    return !axes;
+}
+
 static NTSTATUS get_hid_device_desc( minidriver *minidriver, DEVICE_OBJECT *device, HIDP_DEVICE_DESC *desc )
 {
     HID_DESCRIPTOR descriptor = {0};
@@ -641,9 +682,39 @@
